"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 a line is either sunny or not; with \\(n\\) lines we can never have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) )**  \n\n   * Choose the first \\(k\\) lines to be the \u201cdiagonal\u2011type\u2019\u2019 sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n     Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)), so all \\(L_i\\) are sunny and they are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n   * The points of the set  \n\n     \\[\n     S:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     that lie on the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\) are exactly those with \\(b\\le k\\).  \n     Indeed, for a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le k\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and\n     \\[\n     a = b + (a-b) = b + (i-1) \\quad\\text{with }i=b,\n     \\]\n     so \\((a,b)\\in L_b\\). Conversely, if \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\) then \\(b=i\\).\n\n   * The remaining points of \\(S\\) are those with \\(b>k\\). There are exactly \\(n-k\\) such horizontal rows:\n     \\[\n     R_j:=\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\},\\qquad j=k+1,\\dots ,n .\n     \\]\n\n   * For each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n     \\[\n     H_j:\\; y=j .\n     \\]\n\n     These lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis). They are distinct and together they cover all points of the rows \\(R_j\\).\n\n   * Altogether we have used \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny horizontal lines \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\); the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\). By the discussion above every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion** \u2013 the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the trivial upper bound this yields the desired set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe have at most \\(n\\) lines in total, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nDefine the following families of lines.\n\n* **Sunny diagonal lines**\n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x-(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is sunny.  \n  The intercepts \\(-(i-1)\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n* **Horizontal lines**\n\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and therefore are not sunny.\n  Their ordinates are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nWe claim that the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Points covered by the sunny lines\n\nTake a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\le k\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le k\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); set \\(i=b\\).  \nThen\n\n\\[\ny = x-(i-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b = a-(b-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a = b+(b-1)=2b-1 .\n\\]\n\nMore directly, substituting \\(i=b\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a-(b-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a = b+(b-1)=2b-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\le n\\), the integer \\(a\\) defined above indeed satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_b\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(k\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_b\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points covered by the horizontal lines\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>k\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), we have \\(b\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor such a \\(b\\) the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) is defined, and clearly \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y=b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Completeness of the covering\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to the case \\(b\\le k\\) or to the case \\(b>k\\); the two preceding subsections show that in the former case the point lies on some \\(L_i\\) and in the latter case on some \\(H_j\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S_n\\quad \\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting sunny lines\n\nBy construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal and therefore not sunny. Hence the configuration uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
