---------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Counting the lines  \n\nThe family of lines we use is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{V_b\\mid b\\in B\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe first part contributes exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe second part contributes \\(|B| = B_{\\max}\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nA direct computation shows\n\n\\[\nk + B_{\\max}\n   = k + \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   = n ,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) have the same parity as \\(n+1-k\\).  Hence \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), and all lines are distinct (different slopes or different abscissae).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4 Verification of the covering condition  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\le k-1\\) then (4) holds, so \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{a-b+1}\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\ge k\\) then, as shown in \u00a72.2, \\(b\\le B_{\\max}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completeness of the description of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the trivial bound (1) this proves that the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"

