            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nBecause we are allowed exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\; y = x-(i-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(D_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); hence each is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(D_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\na-b=i-1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2 Non‑sunny auxiliary lines  \n\nWe introduce four families (two will be used, the other two discarded, depending on \\(k\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_a &: x=a && (a=k+1,\\dots ,n),\\\\\nH_b &: y=b && (b=k+1,\\dots ,n),\\\\\nV'_a &: x=a && (a=1,\\dots ,k),\\\\\nH'_b &: y=b && (b=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLines in \\(V_a\\) and \\(V'_a\\) are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)); those in \\(H_b\\) and \\(H'_b\\) are horizontal (slope \\(0\\)).  Hence none of them is sunny.\n\n#### 2.3 Choice of families  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{V_a\\mid a=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{H'_b\\mid b=1,\\dots ,k\\},\n      &\\text{if }k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{H_b\\mid b=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{V'_a\\mid a=1,\\dots ,k\\},\n      &\\text{if }k<\\lceil n/2\\rceil .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe first sub‑case keeps the vertical lines for the “large‑\\(x\\)” region together with the horizontal lines for the “small‑\\(y\\)” region; the second sub‑case does the opposite.  \n\nEach sub‑case contains exactly \\((n-k)+k=n\\) lines, all distinct from one another and from the diagonals (different slopes or different intercepts).\n\n#### 2.4 Full set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal L . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal C|=k+(n)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them (the diagonals) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal C\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), one of the following mutually exclusive situations occurs.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1.  \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\nThen by (3) \\((a,b)\\) belongs to diagonal \\(D_{a-b+1}\\subset\\mathcal C\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2.  \\(a\\ge k+1\\).\n\n*If the vertical family \\(\\{V_a\\}\\) is present* (first sub‑case of (5)), the point lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\n*If the vertical family is absent* (second sub‑case), we have the horizontal family \\(\\{H_b\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=n-k .\n\\]\n\nIf additionally \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\) (which is present).  \nIf \\(b\\le k\\) then, because we are in the second sub‑case, the vertical “small‑\\(x\\)” family \\(\\{V'_a\\}\\) is present; but \\(a\\ge k+1\\) contradicts \\(a\\le k\\). Hence the sub‑case \\(b\\le k\\) cannot occur, and therefore \\(b\\ge k+1\\) and the point is covered by \\(H_b\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 3.  \\(b\\ge k+1\\).\n\nSymmetric to Case 2.  If the horizontal family \\(\\{H_b\\}\\) is present the point lies on \\(H_b\\); otherwise the vertical family \\(\\{V_a\\}\\) is present and the inequality forces \\(a\\le n-k\\) and consequently \\(a\\ge k+1\\), so the point lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 4.  \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b\\le k\\).\n\nHere \\(a-b\\) is negative, so Case 1 does not apply.\n\n*If the first sub‑case of (5) is used* (\\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)), the horizontal “small‑\\(y\\)” lines \\(\\{H'_b\\}\\) are present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H'_b\\).\n\n*If the second sub‑case of (5) is used* (\\(k<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)), the vertical “small‑\\(x\\)” lines \\(\\{V'_a\\}\\) are present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V'_a\\).\n\nThus the point is covered in either situation.\n\n---\n\nSince the four cases exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\), every required lattice point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have constructed a set \\(\\mathcal C\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (1), the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
