            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT_n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}:\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{2}:\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{3}:\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a line of a given direction  \n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on it then \\(b=c\\).  Since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and\n\\(b\\le n\\), the only possible values are \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus\nthere are at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\) on that line.  In particular,\na horizontal line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\) whose\n\\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(c\\).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nThe same argument with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged shows that a\nvertical line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  For \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we need\n\\(a+b=c\\) and also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(c\\le n+1\\).  For each admissible\n\\(c\\) there is exactly one point of \\(S_n\\) with that sum, namely\n\\((a,b)=(c-1,1)\\) after ordering, so a line of slope \\(-1\\) contains at most\none point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line whose direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections.  Then its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  Consequently\n\\(L\\) meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point and each horizontal\nline \\(y=b\\) in at most one point.  Hence among the points of \\(S_n\\) it can\ncontain at most one with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate and at most one with a given\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any sunny line contains at most two points of }S_n.}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, by (1) they could cover at most \\(2n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Since  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(S_n\\) could not be covered.  Therefore at least one line must be\nnon‑sunny.\n\nA single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\):\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((a,1)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);\n* the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains the \\(n\\) points\n  \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\n\nSince \\(T_n>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), one non‑sunny line is insufficient; **two**\nnon‑sunny lines are required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Two non‑sunny lines already cover all of \\(S_n\\)\n\nTake the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  Their union\ncontains the \\(2n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 2n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nevery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to this union.  Hence any configuration that\nincludes at least these two non‑sunny lines automatically satisfies the\ncovering condition, regardless of the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nSince at least two lines must be non‑sunny, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\nobeys  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe construct, for an arbitrary integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\), a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required properties.\n\n1. **Two non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   As shown above, they already cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that avoid all points of \\(S_n\\); for\n   instance the lines \\(y=-m\\) with distinct integers \\(m\\ge 2\\) are sunny\n   (they are horizontal, hence non‑sunny, so we must avoid them).  \n   Instead take lines of slope \\(2\\) passing through points far away, e.g.  \n   \\(L_i:\\; y=2x-(10^3+i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   Their slopes are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so they are sunny,\n   and because the intercepts are large negative numbers none of the points\n   of \\(S_n\\) lies on any \\(L_i\\).\n\n3. **Remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines (if any).**  \n   If \\(k<n-2\\), add further distinct sunny lines that also avoid \\(S_n\\), for\n   example lines \\(y=2x-(10^3+k+j)\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-2-k\\).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the covering\ncondition holds because the two non‑sunny lines already contain every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition derived from counting arguments is \\(k\\le n-2\\); the\nexplicit construction shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Hence,\nfor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the non‑negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two stated requirements. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
