red would be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot (n-1)=n^{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{(by (2))},\n\\]\n\nso the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe remaining case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly.  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) has six points.\nA sunny line must have slope \\(1\\) or any value different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  The only slopes that can pass through two points of\n\\(T_3\\) are \\(1\\) (giving the lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\)) and \\(-2\\) (giving\n\\(y=-2x+5\\)), each of which contains at most two points of \\(T_3\\).  With\nthree such lines the maximum number of covered points is \\(3\\cdot2=6\\),\nbut a simple case analysis shows that no choice of three sunny lines\ncovers all six points simultaneously.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently **\\(k=n\\) never occurs**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i+1,2),\\dots ,\\;(n+1-i,\\;n-i),\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nall of which satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nC_k=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince we removed exactly \\(k\\) integers from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), we have\n\\(|C_k|=n-k\\).  \nFor each \\(c\\in C_k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(M_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe constants \\(c\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(\\{M_c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}\\)\nare distinct.\n\n#### 3.3  The whole family\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{M_c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines (the slopes of\nthe two sub\u2011families are different, so no line appears in both).\n\n#### 3.4  Covering every point of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b=i-1\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)*, then \\(b=a-i+1\\) and\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (3); hence it lies on the sunny line\n\\(L_i\\).\n\n*Otherwise* \\(a-b\\neq i-1\\) for all \\(i\\le k\\).  \nLet \\(c:=a+b\\).  Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\nSince \\(c\\neq i+1\\) for any \\(i\\le k\\) (otherwise \\(a-b=i-1\\) would hold),\n\\(c\\) belongs to \\(C_k\\).  Consequently the line \\(M_c\\) is present in\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=c\\); thus it lies on\n\\(M_c\\).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the construction above\n  provides \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover\n  all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible, as shown in Section\u202f2.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]"
