iv -qb\\pmod q\\); because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) this congruence fixes\n   \\(x\\) modulo \\(|p|\\).  Consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the lattice points\n   on the line form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|p|\\).\n\n   Let \\((x_0,y_0)\\) be the point of the line with smallest \\(x\\)-coordinate that\n   lies in \\(T_n\\).  All other lattice points of the line belonging to \\(T_n\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   (x_0+tp,\\;y_0+tq),\\qquad t=0,1,2,\\dots .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because each such point must satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (x_0+y_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The forbidden slopes guarantee \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\):\n   * \\(p=0\\) would give \\(m=0\\) (horizontal);\n   * \\(p=-q\\) would give \\(m=-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\));\n   * otherwise \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).\n\n   From (3) we obtain\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\le\\frac{n-1}{2},\n   \\]\n   because \\(x_0+y_0\\ge 2\\) and \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  Hence the number of admissible\n   integers \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\), and together with\n   the initial point (\\(t=0\\)) the line contains at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n4. **At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary.**  \n\n   *Lemma 3.*  \n   In any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) at least two lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose at most one line is non‑sunny; then at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\)\n   points, so the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Lemma 2 gives \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  Equality in (4) would require\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), i.e. \\(n\\) odd, and every\n   sunny line would have to attain the bound of Lemma 1.  Lemma 1 shows that a\n   sunny line attains the bound only when \\(|p+q|=2\\), which forces\n   \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\) (the case \\((-1,-1)\\) gives slope \\(-1\\), forbidden).  Hence the\n   only possible maximal sunny lines are those of slope \\(1\\) whose step vector is\n   \\((1,1)\\); such a line must pass through \\((1,1)\\) and therefore is the single\n   line \\(y=x\\).  Consequently at most one sunny line can reach the bound, contradicting the assumption that at least \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exist.  Hence at least two lines must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n   Lemma 3 yields the **upper bound**\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Base covering with \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * two non‑sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     X:\\;x=1,\\qquad Y:\\;y=1 ;\n     \\]\n\n   * for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the line  \n     \\[\n     L_c:\\; y = x - c + 1 .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n     The slope of \\(L_c\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   There are exactly \\(2+(n-1)=n\\) lines.  We first show that they cover all points\n   of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(X\\).\n   * If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(Y\\).\n   * Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Set \\(c=a-b+1\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the case \\(c=1\\) would give\n     \\(a=b\\), which is impossible here since both coordinates are at least \\(2\\).\n     Hence \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) and the line \\(L_c\\) is defined.  Substituting \\(c\\) into\n     (6) we obtain\n     \\[\n     y = x - (a-b+1) + 1 = x - a + b .\n     \\]\n     Evaluating at \\((a,b)\\) gives \\(b = a - a + b\\), i.e. the equality holds, so\n     \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   In the family above the number of sunny lines equals \\(n-2\\) (all the \\(L_c\\)’s).\n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines\n   \\(L_c\\) by additional non‑sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal\n   to \\(n\\).\n\n   For each replacement choose a vertical line \\(x=t\\) with a new integer\n   \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that has not been used before.  Such a line is\n   non‑sunny and is distinct from all previously listed lines.  We claim that the\n   covering property is preserved.\n\n   Indeed, suppose we replace \\(L_c\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  Every point\n   that was covered by \\(L_c\\) has the form \\((c+t,\\,1+t)\\) with\n   \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-c\\) (see (6)).  All these points satisfy \\(x\\ge c\\); the\n   particular point \\((c,1)\\) lies on the new vertical line \\(x=c\\), and for\n   \\(t\\ge 1\\) we have \\(x=c+t>c\\), so the point is already covered by one of the\n   other lines \\(L_{c+t}\\) that remain in the family (because we only delete the\n   line \\(L_c\\) itself).  Hence no point loses its covering.\n\n   Performing this replacement exactly \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a family consisting of\n\n   * the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\);\n   * exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines among the remaining \\(L_c\\)’s;\n   * \\(n-2-k\\) new vertical lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\), they are pairwise distinct, and precisely\n   \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The covering argument above shows that every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration\n   exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n\n   * The upper bound (5) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\)\n     sunny lines.\n   * The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n     \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
