            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A canonical covering by \\(n\\) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\,x+2-t . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Covering.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=t\\) for the unique integer \\(t\\) equal to \\(a+b\\) (which lies between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\)). Substituting \\(b=t-a\\) into (1) gives an identity, so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(\\dfrac{t-2}{t-1}\\). For distinct values of \\(t\\) these slopes are distinct, therefore the lines \\(L_t\\) are pairwise different. Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny versus non‑sunny lines in the family\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nFrom (1) we see:\n\n* slope \\(0\\) occurs when \\(t=2\\); then \\(L_2\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\);\n* slope \\(\\infty\\) occurs when \\(t=n+1\\); then \\(L_{n+1}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=n\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\) occurs when \\(t=3\\); then \\(L_3\\) is the line \\(x+y=2\\).\n\nThus the three lines \\(L_2, L_3, L_{n+1}\\) are non‑sunny, while the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are sunny.  \nHence the configuration \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=2}^{n+1}\\) yields a covering with  \n\n\\[\nk_0=n-3\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Turning non‑sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nEach of the three non‑sunny lines contains **exactly one** point of \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\nL_2\\cap T_n=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad\nL_3\\cap T_n=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad\nL_{n+1}\\cap T_n=\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore we may replace any of them by any other line that passes through the same lattice point(s) and whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the covering property will be preserved.\n\n*Replace \\(L_2\\) (horizontal) by*  \n\n\\[\nS_2:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_2\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(\\frac12\\), which is sunny.\n\n*Replace \\(L_{n+1}\\) (vertical) by*  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}:\\; x=n-\\tfrac13y+\\tfrac23 .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_{n+1}\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(-\\tfrac13\\), which is sunny.\n\n*Replace \\(L_3\\) (diagonal) by*  \n\n\\[\nS_3:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 .\n\\]\n\n\\(S_3\\) also passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq-1\\).\n\nPerforming any \\(r\\) of these replacements (with \\(0\\le r\\le3\\)) turns exactly \\(r\\) formerly non‑sunny lines into sunny ones, while the other \\(n-r\\) lines remain as in the original family. Consequently we obtain coverings with\n\n\\[\nk=n-3+r\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A lower bound