\\(0\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence\n\\(L\\) contains at most \\(n+2-b\\le n+2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\ge 0\\), the maximum is attained for \\(b=0\\) and equals \\(n+2\\).\n\nSimilarly a line parallel to \\(\\ell_{y}\\) (vertical) contains at most \\(n+2\\)\npoints of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\), and a line parallel to \\(\\ell_{d}\\) (slope \\(-1\\))\ncontains at most \\(n+1\\) points (the points with a fixed sum \\(a+b=s\\) are\n\\((0,s),(1,s-1),\\dots ,(s,0)\\), and \\(s\\le n+1\\)).\n\nHence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n+2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).**  \n\nA sunny line, having a slope different from the three forbidden ones,\n can intersect each of the three families of parallel lines in at most one\npoint; consequently it can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\nLet the family of \\(n\\) lines contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines.  Counting the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) covered (with multiplicities) we obtain\n\n\\[\n3k+(n-k)(n+2)\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\frac{(n+2)(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n3k+ n^{2}+2n -kn -2k \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+3n+2}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; 2k \\le 2n-4 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; k\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Choose the sunny lines.*  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{k}\\) from\n\\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y-1=\\alpha_{i}(x-1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines pass through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\); they are distinct\nbecause their slopes are distinct, and each is sunny by construction.\n\n*Choose the non‑sunny lines.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH &: y=0,\\\\\nV &: x=0,\\\\\nD &: x+y=n+1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-3\\) the lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{j}: x+y=n+1-j .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;3\\;(\\text{the lines }H,V,D)\\;+\\;(n-k-3)\n   = n .\n\\]\n\nAll non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions,\nhence they are indeed non‑sunny.\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) is covered.*\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n1. If \\(a=0\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V\\);  \n   if \\(b=0\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H\\).\n\n2. If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\n\n3. If \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\), set \\(j=n+1-(a+b)\\).  \n   Then \\(1\\le j\\le n-k-3\\) (because \\(k\\le n-2\\)), and\n   \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1-j\\); hence it lies on \\(D_{j}\\).\n\n4. The point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to every sunny line \\(S_{i}\\); this guarantees\n   that the sunny lines are indeed part of the covering.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed\nlines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nEquation (3) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nConsequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
