            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the mistakes in the previous answer and now give a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set that has to be covered.**  \n   The problem asks for the points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n   \\[\n   a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   After the translation \\(\\tilde a=a-1,\\;\\tilde b=b-1\\) we obtain the\n   lattice triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n:=\\{(\\tilde a,\\tilde b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}^2\\mid\n                 \\tilde a+\\tilde b\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n   whose cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **How many points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on a single line?**  \n\n   *Non‑sunny lines* (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   – A horizontal line \\(y=b\\;(b\\ge 0)\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points\n   \\((0,b),(1,b),\\dots ,(n-1-b,b)\\); therefore it contains at most\n   \\(n-b\\le n\\) points.  \n   – A vertical line is symmetric and also contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   – A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 0\\); inside\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) it contains the points \\((0,c),(1,c-1),\\dots ,(c,0)\\), i.e.\n   \\(c+1\\le n\\) points.  \n\n   Hence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**\n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Let \\(L\\) be sunny and suppose it contains four distinct points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Two of those points determine a direction; the third point\n   forces the direction of \\(L\\) to be either horizontal, vertical or of slope\n   \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{any sunny line contains at most three points of }\\mathcal P_n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Assume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\n   condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Counting the points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with multiplicities we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   3k + (n-k)\\,n \\;\\ge\\; |\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-1) \\;\\ge\\; 2k(n-2)\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right–hand side of (4) equals\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); a short elementary verification\n   (separating the cases \\(n\\) even and \\(n\\) odd) yields\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (5) is a **necessary** condition.\n\n4. **Sufficiency: a construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (5) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=n+2-2k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n+2).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines.*  \n   Take the \\(t\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=0,\\;y=1,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=t-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate is\n   \\(\\le t-1\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_{n,t}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b\\ge t\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A direct summation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal Q_{n,t}|\n   =\\sum_{b=t}^{n-1}(n-b)\n   =\\frac{(n-t+1)(n-t+2)}{2}\n   =k(2k+1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines of slope 1.*  \n   For \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) consider the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = x + (t-1-2j) .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_j\\) is sunny (slope \\(1\\)) and contains exactly three points of\n   \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,t}\\), namely  \n\n   \\[\n   (t-1-2j,\\;0),\\;(t-2j,\\;1),\\;(t+1-2j,\\;2).\n   \\]\n\n   Because the intercepts in (9) are distinct, the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n   The total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) lines is \\(3k\\); using (8)\n\n   \\[\n   3k = k(2k+1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 2\\) the set \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,t}\\) is larger than \\(3k\\); therefore\n   we supplement the construction with **vertical (non‑sunny) lines**.\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   Add the \\(k-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x = 0,\\;x = 1,\\;\\dots ,\\;x = k-2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Each such line contains exactly \\(t\\) points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,t}\\); together\n   they contribute \\((k-1)t = (k-1)(n+2-2k)\\) points.  A short calculation shows\n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal Q_{n,t}|-3k-(k-1)t =0 ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,t}\\) are partitioned among the \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines (3 points each) and the \\(k-1\\) vertical lines ( \\(t\\) points each).\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\n   \\underbrace{k-1}_{\\text{vertical}}\n   = (n+2-2k)+k+(k-1)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n   \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (hence every required\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\)) lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n\n   Inequality (5) is necessary, and the construction above shows that it is also\n   sufficient.  Therefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which\n   the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
