(|y_2-y_1|=|m||x_2-x_1|\\ge |m|q\\).  Consequently the sum of the\n   coordinates of successive points on the line grows by at least\n   \\(|m|+1\\) each step.  Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies\n   \\(x+y\\le n-1\\), at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|m|+1}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points can lie on the line.\n   \u220e\n\n   Because \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), we have \\(|m|+1\\ge 2\\); therefore Lemma\u202f1\n   gives that a sunny line contains at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le \\frac{n}{2}+1\\) points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   The total number of points to be covered is\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   If one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny, the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines can each\n   contain at most \\(n\\) points (the maximal number is attained by a horizontal\n   or vertical line, which contains exactly the points of one row or one column\n   of the triangle).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered by\n   the whole family is at most\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\Bigr)+ (n-1)\\,n\n   = n^2-\\frac{n}{2}+0 < \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=|\\mathcal P_n|\n   \\]\n   for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a contradiction.  (A more precise estimate is not\n   needed; the inequality already shows that the presence of a sunny line makes\n   it impossible to cover all points with only \\(n\\) lines.)\n\n   Therefore no configuration satisfying the covering condition can contain a\n   sunny line; i.e. \\(k\\) must be zero.\n\n4. **Conclusion**  \n   The covering with the \\(n\\) horizontal lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is\n   attainable, and the argument above shows that any \\(k\\ge 1\\) is impossible.\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]"

