d their lengths  \n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three directions\nhorizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the only non‑sunny lines that can contain points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_b &: y=b\\qquad (b=1,\\dots ,n),\\\\\nV_a &: x=a\\qquad (a=1,\\dots ,n),\\\\\nD_c &: x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the line \\(H_b\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence its length (the number of points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) it contains) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(H_b)=n+1-b.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(V_a)=n+1-a,\\qquad \n\\ell(D_c)=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus each integer \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) occurs exactly three times among the\nlengths of all non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines contain at most two required points  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\\text{If a line is sunny, it contains at most two points of }\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).\nAmong any two of them the slope is either \\(0\\) (if the two have the same\n\\(b\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if they have the same \\(a\\)), or \\(-1\\) (if the sum of the\ncoordinates is the same).  Hence at least one of the three slopes equals\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which means that \\(L\\) is parallel to one of the three\nforbidden directions, contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contribute at most two new points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) to a covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\nand contains exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.  Let the remaining \\(n-s\\) lines be\nnon‑sunny and let their lengths (numbers of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) they\ncover) be \\(\\ell_1\\ge\\ell_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\ell_{\\,n-s}\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-s}\\ell_i+2s\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTo make the left‑hand side of (5) as large as possible for a given \\(s\\) we\nchoose the \\(n-s\\) longest non‑sunny lines.  Because each integer\n\\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) appears three times among the lengths, the multiset of\nall \\(3n\\) lengths is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{n,n,\\dots ,n}_{3\\text{ times}},\n\\underbrace{n-1,n-1,\\dots ,n-1}_{3\\text{ times}},\\dots ,\n\\underbrace{1,1,1}_{3\\text{ times}} .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(m=n-s\\).  Write \\(m=3q+r\\) with \\(q=\\lfloor m/3\\rfloor\\) and\n\\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  The sum of the \\(m\\) largest lengths is then\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(m)&=3\\sum_{j=n-q+1}^{n}j\n      +\\begin{cases}\n          0, & r=0,\\\\[2mm]\n          n-q, & r=1,\\\\[2mm]\n          2(n-q)-1, & r=2 .\n        \\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(L(m)\\) for \\(\\sum\\ell_i\\) in (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nL(n-s)+2s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation using (6) (separately for the three possible residues\n\\(r\\)) simplifies (7) to the single inequality  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most\n\\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny part  \n\nTake all horizontal lines \\(H_b\\) for \\(b=1,\\dots ,n\\) **except** the\n\\(k\\) shortest ones, i.e. omit the lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=k .\n\\]\n\nThe omitted horizontals contain respectively  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-k+1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); altogether they contain  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\sum_{j=1}^{k}(n+1-j)=kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontals cover the other\n\\(|\\mathcal P_n|-S\\) points.\n\n#### 4.2  Sunny part  \n\nThe points that are still uncovered after Step 4.1 are exactly those lying\non the omitted short horizontals.  They form the set  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k,\\;1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\,n+1-j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\,n-j).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(U\\) and are distinct.  The line through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\)\nhas slope \\(-1\\); to obtain a sunny line we rotate it by a very small angle\n\\(\\varepsilon>0\\) (the same \\(\\varepsilon\\) for all \\(j\\)).  Because the rotation\nis arbitrarily small, the rotated line still passes through the two original\npoints \\(P_j,Q_j\\); it is no longer parallel to any of the three forbidden\ndirections, hence it is sunny.  Doing this for each \\(j\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct\nsunny lines, each covering exactly the two points of the corresponding pair.\n\nSince the pairs \\(\\{P_j,Q_j\\}\\) are disjoint, after adding these \\(k\\) sunny\nlines every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.  The total number of lines\nused is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) a configuration\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the universal upper bound (8) with the explicit construction of\nSection 4, we obtain that the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
