es \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) is finite, there exist infinitely many slopes \\(m\\) that are admissible (i.e. \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)).  \nChoose such a slope \\(m\\) that is also different from the slopes of the already constructed lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_t\\) (possible because a finite set of slopes has been used).  \n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{t+1}:\\; y-y_P = m\\,(x-x_P),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\).  \nBy construction \\(S_{t+1}\\) passes through the previously uncovered point \\(P\\), has an admissible slope, and its slope is new, so \\(S_{t+1}\\) is sunny and distinct from the earlier lines.\n\nSet \\(C_{t+1}=C_t\\cup\\{P\\}\\).  \nThus after each step the number of covered points increases by at least one.\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), after at most \\(n\\) steps we have a collection of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=n\n\\]\n\nexists.\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nWe have proved rigorously that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare attainable. The existence of configurations with intermediate numbers of sunny lines (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)) has not been settled by the arguments presented here.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
