use \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a_i\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le b_i\\le n,\\qquad a_i+b_i\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(|b_2-b_1|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1\\).  \nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|=|a_2-a_1|\\) then the slope equals \\(\\pm1\\).  \nThe case \\(+1\\) cannot occur because then the line would be parallel to \\(x-y=0\\), which is not forbidden; however the case \\(-1\\) would make the line non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|\\neq|a_2-a_1|\\) then the slope is a rational number different from \\(0,\\pm1,\\infty\\); nevertheless the line would then intersect the integer lattice in infinitely many points, and in particular would contain a third point of \\(S_n\\), contradicting the finiteness of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition: every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(S_n\\); hence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  \nAll remaining \\(|S_n|-k\\) points must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \nSince there are only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-k\\le n-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |S_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  The only way to avoid a contradiction is that the inequality above is never needed; i.e. the bound imposed by Lemma 1 is simply  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nPartition \\(S_n\\) into the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;