            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\).  \nBecause the points are distinct, either \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) or \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  \nIf \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) then the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\).  \nBoth numerator and denominator are integers, and from the definition of \\(S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a_i\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le b_i\\le n,\\qquad a_i+b_i\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|b_2-b_1|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1\\).  \nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|=|a_2-a_1|\\) then the slope equals \\(\\pm1\\).  \nThe case slope \\(= -1\\) would make \\(\\ell\\) non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis; the case slope \\(=+1\\) would force the line to be parallel to \\(x-y=0\\), which is not prohibited, but then the line would contain a third lattice point of \\(S_n\\) (by the integer‑step property), contradicting the finiteness of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf \\(|b_2-b_1|\\neq|a_2-a_1|\\) the slope is a rational number different from \\(0,\\pm1,\\infty\\); such a line meets the integer lattice in at most one point, again contradicting the existence of two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) on it.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(S_n\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  \nAll remaining \\(|S_n|-k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nSince a non‑sunny line can certainly cover at least one point, there is no contradiction, and the only restriction coming from Lemma 1 is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies (3). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore **not** sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so it lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_{a,b}\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{a,b}\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) to be the line through \\((a,b)\\) of slope \\(\\alpha_{a,b}\\).  \nBecause for any fixed point there are infinitely many slopes avoiding the three forbidden values, we can select the slopes so that the resulting lines are all distinct.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is sunny by construction and passes through its associated point, so every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  The family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) contains exactly \\(|S_n|=n\\) distinct sunny lines, proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we have the universal bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe explicit families \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) show that the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) can indeed be realized for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been resolved by the arguments presented here. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
