hcal P_n\\) is the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices\n   \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  Any line intersecting this triangle meets it in a (possibly\n   degenerate) segment whose endpoints lie on the boundary of the triangle.\n   The longest lattice segment that can appear on the boundary is a side of the\n   triangle; each side contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points, namely the lines listed\n   in (2).  If the line is not parallel to a side, the intersection segment is\n   strictly shorter, so it contains at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points.  ∎\n\n4. **Incidence counting bound**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal\n   P_n\\).  Denote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by\n   \\(\\iota(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\) the number of covered points on a line\n   \\(\\ell\\).  By Lemma 1,\n   \\[\n   \\iota(\\ell)\\le n\\quad\\text{for every }\\ell,\\qquad\n   \\iota(\\ell)\\le n-1\\ \\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is sunny}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Summing (3) over all lines gives\n   \\[\n   I:=\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\iota(\\ell)\\le (n-s)n+s(n-1)=n^{2}-s. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n   \\[\n   I\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Combining (4) and (5) yields\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, (6) imposes **no restriction** on\n   \\(s\\); any integer \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) is compatible with the counting bound.\n\n5. **Correct construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Take the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\qquad\\bigl(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   These are non‑sunny and together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny diagonal lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y = x - (n-k+t-1). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny (the\n   forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  The line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) contains the\n   lattice points\n   \\[\n   (a,\\,a-(n-k+t-1))\\qquad\\text{for }a=n-k+t,\\;n-k+t+1,\\dots ,n .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge n-k+t\\), we have\n   \\[\n   b:=a-(n-k+t-1)\\ge 1,\n   \\qquad\n   a+b = 2a-(n-k+t-1)\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n   the latter inequality being equivalent to \\(a\\le n\\).  Hence every point\n   listed in (9) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Moreover, for any point\n   \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   d:=a-b\\in\\{\\,n-k,\\,n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   Choosing \\(t=d-(n-k)+1\\) (so that \\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) we obtain\n   \\(a-b=n-k+t-1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  Consequently **every**\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate is at least \\(n-k+1\\) is\n   covered by one of the lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – counting the lines.*  \n   The collection consists of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (7) and the \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines (8).  All these lines are distinct: the vertical lines have undefined\n   slope, whereas the \\(\\ell_{t}\\) have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts\n   \\(-\\bigl(n-k+t-1\\bigr)\\).  Hence we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of\n   which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of coverage**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) from (7).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)*, set \\(d=a-b\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have\n   \\(0\\le d\\le n-1\\) and, moreover, \\(d\\ge n-k\\).  Write \\(d=n-k+t-1\\) with\n   \\(1\\le t\\le k\\); then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a-(n-k+t-1)\\) and therefore\n   belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) defined in (8).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one of the\n   constructed lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The counting argument shows that no value of \\(k\\) is ruled out, and the\n   explicit construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
