 and \\(t\\), then \\(b=n+1-a\\), so (4) describes **all** integer points of \\(L_t\\).\n\nComparing (2) and (4) we see that the set of points of \\(L_t\\) is exactly the\n\\(t\\) points of \\(S_k\\) having second coordinate \\(b=n-t+1\\).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the whole covering\n\nThe total collection of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a\nhorizontal line (if \\(b\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (if\n\\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)).  Hence the covering condition of the problem is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Number of sunny lines\n\nExactly the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the horizontals are not.\nThus the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling both requirements of the problem, and\nno larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
