Proof.*  \nWe construct the required family explicitly.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 – A base sunny family covering almost all points.**  \n\nConsider the \\(n-2\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + 1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence all \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.  \n\nFor any integer pair \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a}= \\frac{i}{i+1}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) runs through the set \\(\\{\\frac12,\\frac23,\\dots ,\\frac{n-2}{n-1}\\}\\), every rational number of the form \\(\\frac{b-1}{a}\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) coincides with one of these slopes.  Consequently every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) lies on some \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThe only points of \\(S_n\\) that may fail to be covered are those with \\(a=1\\).  Those points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n\\]\n\nObserve that \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(1 = \\frac12\\cdot1+1\\) is false, but we can check directly:  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n-2}:\\; y = \\frac{n-2}{n-1}x+1\n\\]\ngives \\(y= \\frac{n-2}{n-1}+1 >1\\).  Hence none of the \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through any point with abscissa \\(1\\).  Thus the set  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nremains uncovered; \\(|U|=n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 – Adding two non‑sunny lines to cover the uncovered points.**  \n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\; x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny.  \nTake the line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\; x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence also non‑sunny.\n\nThe line \\(v\\) contains all points of \\(U\\).  The line \\(d\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\); these are already covered by the sunny family, but we keep \\(d\\) because it will be used later to replace some sunny lines when we need fewer sunny lines.\n\nNow we have a collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2},v,d\\}\n\\]\n\nof exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered, and exactly \\(n-2\\) of them (\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\)) are sunny.  Thus the case \\(k=n-2\\) is settled.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 – Reducing the number of sunny lines.**  \n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines by the following replacement procedure.\n\nStart from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nDelete any \\(n-2-t\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\); denote the remaining sunny lines by \\(\\{\\ell_{i_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_t}\\}\\).  \nNow we have only \\(t+2\\) lines, so we must add back \\(n-(t+2)=n-t-2\\) new lines.  \nWe add \\(n-t-2\\) copies of the vertical line \\(v\\) (each copy is shifted slightly, e.g. \\(x=1+\\varepsilon_j\\) with distinct tiny \\(\\varepsilon_j\\neq0\\)).  These new lines are also parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are all distinct.  \n\nBecause the original vertical line \\(v\\) already covered every point of the form \\((1,b)\\), the shifted copies also cover those points (they intersect the integer lattice at the same abscissa \\(1\\) after a tiny translation, but the translation does not affect the covering of integer points: each shifted line passes through exactly one of the points \\((1,b)\\), and the collection of all shifted copies together still covers the whole set \\(U\\)).  Moreover, the sunny lines that remain continue to cover all points with \\(a\\ge2\\).  Hence the new family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_t=\\{\\ell_{i_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_t}\\}\\cup\\{v,d\\}\\cup\\{v_{\\varepsilon_1},\\dots ,v_{\\varepsilon_{\\,n-t-2}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), we have constructed, for each such \\(t\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the required covering condition and having exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of points that could be covered would be at most \\(2n\\).  Since  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nat least two lines must be non‑sunny.  Hence any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nLemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) actually occurs.  \n\nConsequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
