"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* shows that a sunny line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)) can contain **at most two** points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since the total number of points that can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\), the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines (which may be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions) must cover the rest.  \n   Because \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2(n-1)\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny; hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f2* gives an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The construction proceeds as follows.  \n\n   - Start with the \u201csunny backbone\u2019\u2019 consisting of the \\(n-2\\) lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\n     \\]\n\n     all of which have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \n     These lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(2\\).  \n   - The points with \\(x=1\\) (there are exactly \\(n\\) of them) are not covered yet.  \n     They are covered by adding two non\u2011sunny lines:  \n\n     \\[\n     v:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical, parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis)},\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     d:\\;x+y=n+1\\qquad\\text{(parallel to the line }x+y=0\\text{)}.\n     \\]\n\n     The set  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n-2}= \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2},v,d\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(S_n\\), and has exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - To obtain fewer sunny lines, we simply replace some of the sunny backbone lines by additional copies of the vertical line \\(v\\) (each copy shifted by a tiny distinct amount so that the lines remain distinct).  \n     Because the vertical direction is non\u2011sunny, each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property (the vertical copies together still cover all points with \\(x=1\\)).  \n\n   - Performing this replacement \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{k}= \\{\\ell_{i_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_k}\\}\\cup\\{v,d\\}\\cup\\{v_{\\varepsilon_1},\\dots ,v_{\\varepsilon_{\\,n-2-k}}\\},\n     \\]\n\n     where the \\(v_{\\varepsilon_j}\\) are distinct vertical lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n     \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Since \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), every such value is attainable.\n\nThe two lemmas together give the complete characterisation of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### Notation  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (At most two points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line, then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the case \\(m=\\infty\\) is excluded because a vertical line is not sunny).  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nb_i=m a_i+b\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equations gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nb_3-b_1=m(a_3-a_1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points are distinct, the differences \\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(a_3-a_1\\) are non\u2011zero.  \nDividing (1) by \\(a_2-a_1\\) and (2) by \\(a_3-a_1\\) we obtain the same value \\(m\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nb_i\\le n+1-a_i\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nUsing these inequalities in (3) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-a_2)-(n+1-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{(n+1-a_3)-(n+1-a_1)}{a_3-a_1},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a_1-a_2}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{a_1-a_3}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth fractions equal \\(-1\\); consequently the only way (3) can hold is when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= -1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the slope \\(m=-1\\).  This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Hence no sunny line can contain three distinct points of \\(S_n\\), and the desired bound follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Realisation of every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**Step\u202f1 \u2013 A sunny backbone covering all points with \\(x\\ge2\\).**  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); thus each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the rational number \\(\\dfrac{b-1}{a}\\) belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\dots ,\\frac{n-2}{n-1}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, writing \\(b-1= a\\cdot\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) gives \\(i=\\frac{a(b-1)}{a-(b-1)}\\), and the inequalities above guarantee that \\(i\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\).  \nHence there exists an index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Therefore the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa is at least \\(2\\).\n\n**Step\u202f2 \u2013 Covering the remaining points \\((1,b)\\).**  \nThe points with \\(x=1\\) are  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |U|=n .\n\\]\n\nAdd the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical)},\n\\qquad\nd:\\;x+y=n+1\\qquad\\text{(parallel to }x+y=0\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(v\\) contains all points of \\(U\\); the line \\(d\\) is included only to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-2}:=\\mathcal B\\cup\\{v,d\\}\n\\]\n\nis a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 Reducing the number of sunny lines.**  \nFix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) delete \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny backbone lines; denote the remaining sunny lines by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k:=\\{\\ell_{i_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(k+2\\) lines.  To reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines we add \\(n-2-k\\) new vertical lines, each parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis but slightly shifted so that they are distinct:\n\n\\[\nv_{\\varepsilon_j}:\\;x=1+\\varepsilon_j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-2-k),\n\\]\n\nwhere the numbers \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) are non\u2011zero and pairwise distinct (for instance \\(\\varepsilon_j=j\\cdot10^{-3}\\)).  \nAll these added lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\{v,d\\}\\cup\\{v_{\\varepsilon_1},\\dots ,v_{\\varepsilon_{\\,n-2-k}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n*Why does \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) still cover \\(S_n\\)?*  \n\n- Every point with \\(x\\ge2\\) is covered by one of the remaining sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i_j}\\) (the argument of Step\u202f1 uses only the slope, not the particular index).  \n- Every point of \\(U\\) has abscissa \\(1\\).  The original vertical line \\(v\\) covers all of them; the shifted vertical lines \\(v_{\\varepsilon_j}\\) each intersect the integer lattice at a point with abscissa \\(1\\) (namely \\((1,b)\\) for a suitable \\(b\\)), and together they still cover the whole set \\(U\\).  (Indeed, for each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we may choose \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) so that the line \\(v_{\\varepsilon_j}\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\); the explicit choice of the \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) is irrelevant because the presence of the original line \\(v\\) already guarantees coverage.)  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n\nSince \\(k\\) was arbitrary in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\), we have constructed, for each such \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\n*Upper bound.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nHence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Existence.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nCombining the two parts, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
