"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Trivial upper bound.**  \n   With exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines we can never have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n   Write \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   *Non\u2011sunny lines*: the vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\).  \n   *Sunny lines*: for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x + (n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise distinct, so the \\(S_j\\) are distinct lines.  Together we have \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n3. **Verification that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.**  \n\n   *Case\u202f1 \u2013 \\(1\\le a\\le m\\).*  \n   The point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (since \\(x=a\\)).  \n\n   *Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\).*  \n   Write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(m+j)=k+1-j .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a=m+j\\) into the equation of \\(S_j\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y_{0}= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(m+j)+(n+1)=k+1-j .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,\\,k+1-j)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \n   Because the slope of \\(S_j\\) is negative, the line contains **all** points with the same abscissa \\(a\\) whose ordinate is **at most** \\(k+1-j\\).  Inequality (1) shows that the ordinate \\(b\\) of our lattice point satisfies exactly this condition, therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Consequently every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered either by a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(m\\)) or by one of the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\)).\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   By construction the configuration contains precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\); all other \\(m=n-k\\) lines are vertical and therefore not sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Together with the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\), this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  A line is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).  Non\u2011negativity is obvious. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) set \\(m:=n-k\\) and consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{V_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,m\\}\\cup\\{S_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i ,\\qquad \nS_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x + (n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) are distinct and none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, while each \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny.  The lines are pairwise distinct, so \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Coverage)\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a\\le m\\):* the point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\) because that line is defined by \\(x=a\\).\n\n*If \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\):* write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(j:=a-m\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(m+j)=k+1-j .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the equation of \\(S_j\\) at \\(x=a=m+j\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{0}\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(m+j)+(n+1)\\\\\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(n-k+j)+(n+1) \\qquad(\\text{since }m=n-k)\\\\\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(n-j)+(n+1)\\\\\n&= \\frac{(n+1)(j+1)-j(n-j)}{j+1}\\\\\n&= \\frac{n + j^{2}+j+1}{j+1}\\\\\n&= k+1-j .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,\\,k+1-j)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(S_j\\) is negative, for the fixed abscissa \\(a\\) the line contains **all** points whose ordinate is at most \\(k+1-j\\).  By (1) the ordinate \\(b\\) of our lattice point satisfies this condition, therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_j\\).\n\nHence every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered either by a vertical line (when \\(a\\le m\\)) or by a sunny line \\(S_j\\) (when \\(a>m\\)). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Number of sunny lines)\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  The \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and thus not sunny.  Each \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  No other line is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Lemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Lemma\u202f2 together with Lemmas\u202f3 and\u202f4 provides, for **every** integer \\(k\\) in this interval, an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that meets the covering requirement and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no other integer can occur. \u220e"
