n does not exist. The actual cardinality of \\(S\\) is\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=2}^{n-1} (n+1-a-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhich equals \\(n-2\\) only for \\(n=3\\). For larger \\(n\\) the set is much larger. Hence the statement “\\(S\\) contains exactly \\(n-2\\) points” is false.\n\nBecause the subsequent construction relies on assigning **one distinct sunny line to each point of \\(S\\)**, the erroneous count leads to an incorrect number of sunny lines in the constructed family.\n\n#### Assignment of a sunny line to each \\((a,b)\\in S\\)\n\nThe solution proposes an injection\n\\[\n\\phi:S\\longrightarrow\\{\\text{lines of slope }\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\nand defines \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) as the image of \\((a,b)\\).  \nAssuming such an injection exists (which is plausible because there are infinitely many slopes), each \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) is sunny and distinct. This part is acceptable **provided** the set \\(S\\) is correctly identified.\n\n#### Definition of the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{\\ell_{v},\\ell_{h}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the counting error, \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}| = 2+|S|\\) is **not** equal to \\(n\\) in general; for \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|>n\\). Thus the construction does **not** produce a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines, violating the problem’s requirement.\n\n#### Coverage verification\n\nThe argument that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) is correct **if** the family actually contained the required lines. However, because the family size is wrong, the verification is moot.\n\n#### Reducing the number of sunny lines\n\nThe author writes:\n\n> “To obtain configurations with fewer sunny lines, simply delete any subset of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) while keeping the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_{v},\\ell_{h}\\). … Consequently the point is still covered by \\(\\ell_{v}\\) or \\(\\ell_{h}\\).”\n\n**Critical 