 take the line of slope \\(1\\) that passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x-i+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the points\n   \\[\n   (i+r,\\,1+r)\\qquad (r=0,1,\\dots ,n-i-1),\n   \\]\n   i.e. the whole \u201cdiagonal\u201d that starts at the bottom of column \\(i\\) and proceeds one step up and one step to the right until it leaves the triangle \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *The remaining point.*  \n   The point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=n\\)) is not covered by any of the lines above. It is covered by the line\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   which is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   - Choose any set \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   - For each \\(i\\in I\\) include the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \n   - For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus I\\) include the vertical line \\(V_i\\).  \n   - Include the vertical line \\(V_{n-1}:x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   - Include the diagonal line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   |I|+(n-2-|I|)+1+1=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(|I|=k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n\n   *Points with \\(b\\le 1\\).*  \n   They are of the form \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   - If \\(a=n\\) the point is \\((n,1)\\) and lies on \\(D\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le n-2\\) and \\(a\\in I\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  \n   - Otherwise \\((a,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *Points with \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Write a point as \\((a,b)=(a',a')+(r,\\,b-a')\\) where \\(a'=\\min\\{a,b-1\\}\\).  \n   In the triangle \\(T_n\\) we always have \\(a\\le n-b+1\\).  \n   Two cases occur:\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-2\\) and \\(a\\in I\\).  \n     Then the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) because all points of the form \\((a+r,1+r)\\) belong to \\(S_a\\) and \\(b=1+(b-1)=1+r\\) with \\(r=b-1\\).\n\n   - **Case 2.** Otherwise the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (if \\(a\\le n-2\\) and \\(a\\notin I\\)) or on \\(V_{n-1}\\) (if \\(a=n-1\\)).  \n     Indeed, for any \\(a\\) the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-a\\}\\) is exactly the column of points with abscissa \\(a\\); all of them are covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\), and for an arbitrary \\(k\\) we have built a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

